    "## 5. From reconstruction to an anomaly score map\n",
    "The AE reconstructs the **whole image** at once. The anomaly score of a pixel is its squared reconstruction error, averaged over the 3 colour channels.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two post-processing steps turn the raw error into a usable score:\n",
    "1. **Gaussian smoothing.** Raw per-pixel errors are noisy (texture, edges); smoothing turns them into region-like scores. The strength `sigma` is chosen on **validation** with the main metric (AuPRC).\n",
    "2. **Per-image standardisation** (z-score). Texture-rich images (foliage, buildings) have a high error *everywhere*, so one global threshold would flag those whole images. Standardising each error map turns the score into \"how unusual is this pixel **compared to the rest of its own image**\". Limitation: this assumes that an image is mostly normal. On an image with no obstacle at all, its highest-error pixels would still stand out."
   ]
  },
