    "## 11. Results and critical analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**Summary (validation was used for every choice; test was used once, at the end)**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Validation | Test | Test 95 % CI | Chance / trivial reference |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| AuPRC (main) | 0.148 | 0.177 | — | prevalence: 0.091 (val), 0.086 (test) |\n",
    "| AUROC | 0.677 | 0.664 | — | 0.5 |\n",
    "| AUPRO @ FPR 0.3 | 0.506 | 0.526 | — | 0.15 |\n",
    "| Pixel F1 = Dice, per-image mean | 0.234 | 0.245 | 0.117 – 0.388 | \"all anomaly\": 0.159 (val) |\n",
    "| Pixel IoU, per-image mean | 0.138 | 0.158 | 0.070 – 0.262 | \"all anomaly\": 0.091 (val) |\n",
    "| Precision / Recall, per-image mean | 0.169 / 0.569 | 0.178 / 0.505 | — | — |\n",
    "| Object recall (≥ 50 % covered) | 25/36 | 16/22 | — | — |\n",
    "| Object false alarms / predicted blobs | 52/65 | 36/42 | — | — |\n",
    "\n",
    "Validation and test agree, so the threshold and smoothing chosen on validation transfer to unseen scenes. The test split contains no small object (section 9: small 0/0), so the small-object recall can only be judged on validation (9/12).\n",
    "\n",
    "**What works.** Obstacles whose **colour or contrast differs from a typical road scene** get a high error: the zebras (F1 0.60), the skid-loader and cones (0.50), the traffic cones (0.29, all 6 covered), and the donkeys (0.46).\n",
    "\n",
    "**What fails, and why.** The AE has no notion of *object*. It only knows *typical local appearance*:\n",
    "- **False negatives:** obstacles with ordinary colours and textures, such as the grey boulders on a grey road (`obstacles09_boulder3`: F1 0.00) or the brown boar on brown leaf litter (`animals20`: 0.01), are reconstructed well, so their error stays low.\n",
    "- **False positives:** rich background textures such as foliage, leaf litter, building facades and sky gradients are reconstructed badly, so they score as high as real obstacles. This is the main source of the 36 false alarms on test.\n",
    "- Pixel reconstruction error measures *\"unusual appearance\"*, not *\"unexpected object on the road\"*. That gap is the core limitation of a pixel-space autoencoder on varied, real-world internet photos.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations to keep in mind**\n",
    "1. **Small evaluation sets.** With 9 test images the F1 interval is wide (0.12 – 0.39). Differences with the other binômes' models are meaningful only when the intervals do not overlap.\n",
    "2. **Per-image standardisation** assumes most of an image is normal. On an image with no obstacle at all, its highest-error pixels would still be flagged (every RoadAnomaly image contains an obstacle, so this case is not tested here).\n",
    "3. **Normal data comes from the same scenes.** The AE learns \"normal\" from background patches of images that also contain obstacles. A larger set of truly obstacle-free roads would give a better model of normality; per FR it needs an explicit authorisation.\n",
    "4. **Smoothing.** The chosen `sigma = 16` is the largest value tried. Heavy smoothing makes region-like blobs, which helps the object recall (≥ 50 % coverage) but blurs the boundaries.\n",
    "5. **Transparency.** The first version of the model (section 3.1) was also evaluated on test once, before it was replaced on validation evidence. Its test AuPRC was 0.192 and its F1 0.198.\n",
    "\n",
    "**XAI note (FR, section 5).** The error map is a **detection map**: it shows *where* the reconstruction fails. It is **not an attribution**, and it does not explain *why* the model \"decided\" anything, because the AE makes no decision about obstacles at all. Any Grad-CAM added later must target a defined scalar, for example the mean error inside the predicted mask, and its faithfulness should be checked with deletion / insertion tests."
   ]
  },
