   "source": [
    "## 13. v2 results and critical analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**Summary (every v2 choice made on validation; test used once, in 12.8)**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | v1 val | v2 val | v1 test | v2 test | v2 test 95 % CI | Chance / trivial reference |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| AuPRC (main) | 0.148 | 0.596 | 0.177 | **0.631** | — | prevalence: 0.091 (val), 0.086 (test) |\n",
    "| AUROC | 0.677 | 0.913 | 0.663 | **0.922** | — | 0.5 |\n",
    "| AUPRO @ FPR 0.3 | 0.506 | 0.700 | 0.526 | **0.823** | — | 0.15 |\n",
    "| Pixel F1 = Dice, per-image mean | 0.234 | 0.575 | 0.245 | **0.623** | 0.506 – 0.729 | \"all anomaly\": 0.159 (val) |\n",
    "| Pixel IoU, per-image mean | 0.138 | 0.421 | 0.158 | **0.474** | 0.355 – 0.583 | \"all anomaly\": 0.091 (val) |\n",
    "| Precision / Recall, per-image mean | 0.169 / 0.569 | 0.575 / 0.710 | 0.178 / 0.505 | 0.590 / 0.769 | — | — |\n",
    "| Object recall (≥ 50 % covered) | 25/36 | 19/36 | 16/22 | 16/22 | — | — |\n",
    "| Object false alarms / predicted blobs | 52/65 | 17/29 | 36/42 | **10/22** | — | — |\n",
    "\n",
    "Final v2 configuration: frozen VGG16 `block5_conv3` features (standardised with train statistics), feature autoencoder with an 8-channel linear code, a single seed, sigma = 8, no road prior, threshold 0.595 (z), predicted blobs under 256 px removed.\n",
    "\n",
    "The v1 and v2 test F1 intervals do not overlap (0.117 – 0.388 vs 0.506 – 0.729), so the improvement is not a sampling accident of 9 test images. Validation and test also agree (AuPRC 0.596 vs 0.631), so the choices made on validation transfer to unseen scenes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What changed, and why it works.** In ImageNet feature space an obstacle and a road look different even when their colours are the same. The two worst v1 failures are now among the best v2 images: the grey boulder on the grey road (`obstacles09_boulder3`, F1 0.00 → 0.83) and the brown boar on brown leaves (`animals20`, 0.01 → 0.77). Texture no longer dominates the score: false alarms on test fall from 36 of 42 predicted blobs to 10 of 22.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where the gain comes from (the most important point of this section).** The control experiments show that **most of the improvement comes from the pretrained features, not from the autoencoder**:\n",
    "- the perceptual error of the v1 pixel AE scores the same as the plain feature norm, with no reconstruction at all (12.2);\n",
    "- at the chosen depth `block5`, the **features without any AE score a little higher than the feature AE** on validation (AuPRC 0.627 vs 0.596, 12.6). At `block4` the AE adds a little (0.430 vs 0.418), and at `block3` it removes a little.\n",
    "\n",
    "With 42 training images, the autoencoder cannot learn \"what a normal road looks like\" better than ImageNet pre-training already encodes \"what an object looks like\". This is reported as a finding, not hidden. The control was evaluated on validation only and is not a submitted model. In the group comparison, the v2 numbers must be read as **\"pretrained features + feature AE\"**, and the role of the pre-training must be stated.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Components that were tried and dropped (validation evidence)**\n",
    "- **Road prior:** AuPRC 0.596 → 0.580. The top 12 % of the image holds 1 % of the train anomaly pixels but 3.25 % of the validation ones: the bear and the Czech hedgehog are close to the camera and reach the top of the frame. A fixed geometric prior is not safe on photos taken from varied viewpoints.\n",
    "- **3-seed ensemble:** no change (0.596 → 0.596). The feature AE is already stable across seeds, so averaging has no variance left to remove.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What still fails**\n",
    "- **Large obstacles** (`obstacles10_boulder4`, 19 % of the image, F1 0.40): only parts of the boulder score high. The per-image z-score assumes the obstacle is a small part of the image, and the inside of a large rock looks like ordinary rocky ground.\n",
    "- **Objects that are not labelled as anomalies** (`cones06`: bollards and pedestrians; `vehicle02`: the house): the features detect *objects*, not *unexpected objects on the road*. VGG has no notion of what belongs in a road scene.\n",
    "- **Small objects:** validation small-object recall drops from 9/12 (v1) to 4/12. A `block5` cell covers 16x16 px and the chosen minimum blob is 256 px, so the method trades small obstacles for far fewer false alarms. The test split has no small object, so this weakness cannot appear in the test numbers and must be kept in mind.\n",
    "- **Coarse boundaries:** a 16-px cell blurs the outline: red halos around the animals and blue legs in the figure.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations to keep in mind**\n",
    "1. **Test looks.** Counting both versions, the test split has now been evaluated three times: v1 with a ReLU code, v1 with a linear code, and v2. Every choice was made on validation, and each change is documented with its validation evidence.\n",
    "2. **Pre-trained weights are external knowledge.** VGG16 was trained on ImageNet, not on RoadAnomaly. This is the same kind of transfer learning as Binôme A's backbone, but it must be stated in the report, and confirmed as allowed for Binôme B.\n",
    "3. **Small evaluation sets** (9 + 9 images): the intervals are still wide.\n",
    "4. **Per-image standardisation** still assumes that each image contains an obstacle (true for every RoadAnomaly image).\n",
    "\n",
    "**XAI note.** As in v1, the score map is a **detection map** (where the features are not reconstructed), not an attribution of a decision."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "014fdd5c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 14. Final check: stronger backbones and a density control (validation only)\n",
    "Two questions remain after v2:\n",
    "1. **Is VGG16 the right feature extractor?** It is an old network (2014). Ten other ImageNet-pretrained encoders from `keras.applications` are compared: VGG19, three ResNets, DenseNet121, Xception, MobileNetV3, two EfficientNets and ConvNeXt-Tiny. They are pretrained **encoders**, not pretrained autoencoders: Keras offers no ImageNet-pretrained autoencoder, so each one is used exactly like VGG16 in v2, as the frozen feature space of our feature AE. InceptionV3 is left out because its valid padding gives no 22x40 layer.\n",
    "2. **Does the autoencoder really learn \"normal road\"?** Section 12 compared it with the standardised feature norm, which is a Gaussian with a **diagonal** covariance. The fair control for an AE is a Gaussian with a **full** covariance, scored with the Mahalanobis distance (the idea of PaDiM, Defard et al. 2020).\n",
    "\n",
    "PaDiM fits one Gaussian per image position because its images are aligned (industrial parts). Our photos are not aligned and each position has only 42 train images, so **one Gaussian is fitted on all the train normal cells**. With 512 to 1024 channels, the covariance is estimated with **Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage**, which stays invertible even when channels are strongly correlated.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Decision rule, fixed before the run.** Per FR, the model of Binôme B is the autoencoder, so the final model is the **best feature AE on validation**. The density models are controls and never see the test split. If the best AE is still VGG16 (v2), the test split is **not used again**."
   ]
  },
